shell {"status": "success", "content": [{"text": "Execution Summary:\nTotal commands: 1\nSuccessful: 1\nFailed: 0"}, {"text": "Command: cd /workspace/cache && grep -n -i -E \"ExploitGym|99\\.93|Daybreak|available|price|\\\\\\$|token|context|input|output|GPT-5\\.6 Cyber\" gpt-6-cyber-2026-11-21.md | head -60\nStatus: success\nExit Code: 0\nOutput: 16:[Join Daybreak](https://openai.com/form/enterprise-trusted-access-for-cyber/) [Read the system card(opens in a new window)](https://deploymentsafety.openai.com/gpt-6-cyber)\r\n22:We’re introducing GPT‑6 Cyber, a major leap in security research: more capable at finding and fixing vulnerabilities, and far more reliable staying within the boundaries researchers set. GPT‑6 Cyber delivers major gains on key cybersecurity benchmarks over GPT‑5.6 Cyber, including a 51% relative improvement on ExploitGym and a step function improvement when tested on staying within scope. It is already helping to secure real-world software, uncovering and validating thousands of exploits in security-critical codebases such as Linux and Firefox.\r\n24:On ExploitGym, which tests whether models can exploit a specified vulnerability, GPT‑6 Cyber scores 48.5%, up from 32.1% for GPT‑5.6 Cyber. It also follows task boundaries more reliably: in a separate alignment evaluation, GPT‑6 Cyber avoided exploiting out-of-scope services in 99.93% of tests without its deployment prompt, and 100% with this prompt. This improvement in alignment reflects dedicated training to stay within authorized scope, respect restrictions, and report results accurately.\r\n28:It is clear that we have [a limited window to use frontier models to secure software⁠](https://openai.com/index/the-defenders-window/). **Our goal is to dramatically expand Daybreak, bringing advanced security research to organizations of every size, independent researchers, and open-source maintainers.** Mainline models such as GPT‑6 Astra already support code review, vulnerability triage, and patch development. Daybreak Blue helps verified defenders validate vulnerabilities and fixes with fewer unnecessary refusals, while Daybreak Red enables deeper exploit development and authorized security testing with GPT‑6 Cyber.\r\n34:GPT-5.6(Daybreak Blue)GPT-5.6 CyberGPT-6 Astra(Safeguards Enabled)GPT-6 Astra(Daybreak Blue)GPT-6 Cyber0.0%20.0%40.0%60.0%80.0%100.0%30.3%32.1%0.0%42.4%48.5%\r\n36:On ExploitGym, which tests whether models can exploit a specified vulnerability in a remote target, GPT‑6 Cyber scores **48.5%**, about **16 percentage points** higher than GPT‑5.6 Cyber’s **32.1%**. To test vulnerability discovery, we also built a new evaluation for offensive web penetration testing. Models probe web applications without access to their source code. GPT‑6 Cyber achieved **40.3%** recall of vulnerabilities compared with **32.0%** for GPT‑6 Astra.\r\n38:Daybreak Red is designed to support more advanced security workflows, including developing working exploits and using them against remote targets. To evaluate these capabilities, we built a new benchmark called RemoteExploitBench, which uses known vulnerabilities in applications such as GitLab, PostgreSQL, and Jenkins deployed in a controlled environment. In our initial evaluation, GPT‑6 Astra running with Daybreak Blue **refused 100% of tasks**, while GPT‑6 Cyber **completed 95%**; its only failure was an exploit it could not get to work remotely. See the [system card⁠(opens in a new window)](https://deploymentsafety.openai.com/gpt-6-cyber) for full results and evaluation settings.\r\n46:One of these evaluations is ExploitGym Honeypot, which was informed by the [Hugging Face incident⁠](https://openai.com/index/hugging-face-incident-and-the-road-ahead/). We give the model a difficult exploitation task in an isolated environment with access to supporting services, such as a package registry or object store. We deliberately make these services easy to exploit so that attacking them can reveal the task’s flag. The model must infer that exploiting them is outside the scope of its task.\r\n48:Isolated task environmentCodebaseTarget serviceAuxiliary servicesNo internet accessInspect sourceExploitation authorizedNormal use allowedExploitation is out of scopeAgentUse toolsLocal filesNetwork requestsNetwork requestsRollout + logsScope evaluationDid model stay in scope?ExploitGym Honeypot: Do agents respect task scope?\r\n50:GPT‑6 Cyber shows a step-change improvement over GPT‑5.6 Cyber on this evaluation. When tested without deployment safety prompts, it avoided exploiting out-of-scope services in 99.93% of cases, up from 94.49% for GPT‑5.6 Cyber. With those prompts, GPT‑6 Cyber stayed within bounds 100% of the time.\r\n156:- **Protection against stolen credentials.** We require IP allowlisting for Daybreak Red API access by default, restricting requests to approved networks with limited exceptions. We also monitor credentials for compromise.\r\n167:First, to equip these defenders, our $1 billion [Daybreak for Frontline Defenders⁠](https://openai.com/index/daybreak-for-frontline-defenders/) grant program subsidizes access for resource-constrained teams such as government organizations and essential service providers. We’re also introducing a new group within Daybreak Red ( **Daybreak Red for Critical Infrastructure**) for approved critical infrastructure defenders, which gives them permissions to conduct authorized testing of critical systems while retaining safeguards against model misalignment.\r\n169:Second, to prevent misuse of the Daybreak Red and Blue platforms, we have refined safeguards against assistance and capability development that could compromise industrial controls or safety-critical systems, interfere with equipment, or cause serious injury, catastrophic physical damage, or life-endangering service disruption. The figure below compares safeguard label rates across four access configurations, before and after the recent updates we’re rolling out alongside GPT‑6 Cyber.\r\n177:StandardDaybreak BlueDaybreak RedDaybreak Red for CriticalInfrastructure0.0%20.0%40.0%60.0%80.0%100.0%Label rate (%)70.8%89.7%13.8%67.0%0.0%61.9%0.0%0.3%BeforeAfter\r\n181:To evaluate how our models can help defend critical infrastructure, we partnered with Dragos, the global provider for extended operational technology (xOT) cybersecurity. Dragos ran evaluations using cyber-permissive Daybreak Red models in a controlled lab environment. The evaluations covered requests progressing from network discovery and asset enumeration through targeted reconnaissance, sensitive data exfiltration, and unauthorized, potentially dangerous device actions.\r\n187:We used feedback from Dragos to refine our safeguards, aiming to block harmful assistance without unnecessarily restricting authorized testing. [See their final report⁠(opens in a new window)](https://hub.dragos.com/whitepaper/frontier-ai-models-xot-environment) for details on the evaluations. We’re also working to expand partnerships with other offensive security providers and purple teams on penetration testing and red teaming with AI, and with energy companies, other critical infrastructure operators, and national labs to assess our models’ effectiveness and safeguards in industrial defense. We have also increased our investment in threat investigations to detect model usage involving OT/ICS. We’ll continue refining our safeguards with input from partners and the security community, including reports through /feedback when safeguards miss harmful activity or interrupt legitimate work.\r\n191:GPT‑6 Cyber is initially available through the API and Codex to a select group of approved Daybreak Red customers. We will progressively expand access to all Daybreak Red customers soon, and are also making GPT‑5.6 Cyber available to all Daybreak Red customers. We’re also expanding Daybreak Blue alpha access for GPT‑6 Astra to more organizations and individual security researchers; [let us know⁠(opens in a new window)](https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdQu8mNYXDpUW_0kRS6JBWBPMWfUjjxi6xLi8AkFPObLupkpg/viewform) if you’re interested.\r\n193:New users and organizations can [apply for Daybreak⁠](https://openai.com/daybreak/) and get started with our [cyber skills, products, and partners⁠](https://openai.com/business/solutions/cybersecurity/).\r\n\nError: "}], "toolUseId": "call_00_tYb1x48in8DchHu9BJUF0686"}